# The Legal Consequences of Automated Mistake

**Authors:** Simon Gleeson

## Abstract
<p>When the law seeks to determine whether a mistaken contract should be invalidated, it looks at the state of mind of the parties at the time of the contract. However, where a contract is arrived at by algorithms dealing directly with each other, the parties have no states of mind to be considered. This raises the question of what the policy of the law should be when, by error, such algorithms enter into contracts which no reasonable human being would have entered into had he been aware of them. The current state of the law seems to be that no remedy is or should be available. This is unfortunate, since it is a flagrant breach of the general principle that the consequences of a contract should not differ because it is concluded electronically rather than verbally or in writing. This chapter argues that, if the approaches to mistake currently applied provide no remedy in the context of electronic contracts, new approaches need to be developed to provide such remedies.</p>

